WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.790
- Global climate change is
wreaking havoc on this planet.

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:04.930
In the Pacific, many islands
are projected to disappear

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:05.763
like Atlantis.

00:00:05.763 --> 00:00:07.810
In the Arctic, polar ice caps are melting,

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:09.880
in Australia, devastating brush fires

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:10.990
have killed 3 billion animals,

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:13.990
and in America, we're getting
once in a century storms

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:15.050
twice a decade.

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:17.560
And like with most things,
it's up to the lawyers

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:18.960
to save the day.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:20.190
Because whether the world acts

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:21.770
to mitigate the effects of climate change

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:23.740
depends a lot on the state of the law

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:25.830
in the world's most developed nations.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:27.710
And when the law has to change,

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:29.260
who are you gonna call?

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:30.139
Lawyers.

00:00:30.139 --> 00:00:33.020
(light melodic music)

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:34.760
- [Announcer] Brought to you
in partnership with Bill Gates.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:35.593
- Hey, Legal Eagles.

00:00:35.593 --> 00:00:37.710
It's time to think like, well, a lawyer.

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:40.850
Because like it or not, the
lawyers are going to have a role

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:42.410
in fighting climate change,

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:44.430
because climate change
is a global problem,

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:46.240
which demands global solutions.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:48.480
And often emissions that
are made in one area

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.290
affects another place in the world,

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:51.800
if not the world at large.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:54.270
So to really impact the
temperature of the world,

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:56.010
nations have to act in concert

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:57.470
over the long-term for decades.

00:00:57.470 --> 00:00:59.870
However, the ability of the
world to tackle climate change

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:02.310
depends substantially
on how sovereign nations

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:04.620
are constrained by their
own form of government

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.110
and especially their laws.

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:08.920
And not all countries view
climate change the same way.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:10.480
Denmark for instance, passed a law

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:12.490
that made climate change actually illegal.

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:14.260
And now every year, the Danish government

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:16.490
will need to find a majority
parliamentary approval

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:18.480
of its global and national
climate strategies.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.480
The law commits Denmark to
making all major policy decisions

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.900
through a green lens to assess
their impact on climate.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:27.210
And the country has to
share green technologies

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:29.510
with other nations so that
they can reduce emissions.

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:31.650
Eight of the 10 parties
in the Danish parliament

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:34.200
voted for the law which limits
how much future governments

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.190
can undermine it.

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:36.610
But what's possible in Denmark

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:38.420
might not be possible in the U.S.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:39.750
And right now the United States

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:42.960
is not exactly known for
passing bipartisan legislation.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:47.340
So America's strategy may
be more, well, American.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:50.540
Because in America, we
just sue each other.

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:52.000
So, if you're climate mindful

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.930
you probably wonder how
individuals might be able

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:58.210
to pressure the government
to act through lawsuits.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:00.000
And naturally many people have attempted

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.250
to sue fossil fuel
companies for the emissions

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:04.760
that are a by-product of those products.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:08.740
And lawsuits in Europe have
had some success in this area.

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:10.880
Particularly last March, a
U.K. court of appeals said

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:13.450
that plans to expand Heathrow
Airport were unlawful

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:15.490
because they failed to take
the Paris Climate Agreement

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:16.340
into account.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:17.940
But in America, climate change plaintiffs

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:20.460
have yet to find big
success in federal court.

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:22.240
The first major hurdle
for American litigants

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:23.540
is whether cases can satisfy

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:25.170
the threshold procedural requirements

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:27.230
for a court to even decide a case.

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:28.910
These include concepts like standing,

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:31.520
which examines whether
litigants are the right parties

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:32.770
to bring a lawsuit.

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:34.780
Most recent cases brought
by American citizens

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:37.420
have been knocked out at these
early stages of litigation.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:40.150
For example, a 2018 case
brought by Oregon children

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:42.270
initially satisfied a motion to dismiss,

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:44.130
but the ninth circuit
court of appeals reversed.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:45.590
The child plaintiffs asserted

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:48.310
that they had a fifth amendment
right to a climate system

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:50.170
capable of sustaining human life,

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:52.140
but the ninth circuit said
that the kids lack standing

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:53.180
to bring the claims.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:54.330
The court rejected the premise

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:56.310
that courts should decide
climate change issues

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:57.890
saying that only the legislative

00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:00.480
and executive branches of
government have that power.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:02.440
And this isn't terribly surprising.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:04.980
American courts aren't
supposed to set policy.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:07.650
So many U.S. courts have
dismissed lawsuits on the basis

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:09.840
that courts simply
can't set a policy where

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.390
the executive branch has been
given authority to regulate.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:13.360
And that was one of the issues

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:14.900
in the first Supreme court case

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:16.780
to decide whether
corporations could be sued

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:18.210
for greenhouse gas emissions.

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:19.860
American Electric versus Connecticut.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:21.730
The lawsuit was filed by
eight States, New York City,

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:24.400
in three land conservation
groups against the TVA

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:25.570
the Tennessee Valley Authority,

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:27.430
as well as several other power companies.

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:29.050
The plaintiff's alleged
that the defendants

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:31.750
were a public nuisance
because their CO2 emissions

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:33.740
contributed substantially
to global warming.

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:34.830
The Supreme Court held that

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:37.730
only the Environment
Protection Agency, the EPA,

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:39.010
could set emission standards

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:41.220
and that the EPA's authority
prevented plaintiffs

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:43.110
from suing under a legal theory

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:45.970
based on federal common law, nuisance law.

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:48.130
And based on the EPA's
designated authority,

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:50.020
it's up to the EPA to decide whether

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:52.100
and how to regulate
greenhouse gas emissions

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:53.460
from the defendant power plants.

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:55.030
States and cities don't have the right

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:56.650
to sue the utility companies.

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:58.830
In 2021, the Supreme Court will decide

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:00.570
a major climate change case,

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:02.420
but only on the narrow technical issues

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:04.990
about whether the case must
be removed from state court

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:05.823
to federal court.

00:04:05.823 --> 00:04:08.160
The city of Baltimore
filed the lawsuit in 2018

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:10.270
against oil and gas companies
saying that their products

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:12.040
had dangerous impacts on climate

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:13.510
injuring Baltimore citizens.

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:15.570
And defendants filed a
motion to remove the case

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:16.403
to federal court.

00:04:16.403 --> 00:04:18.670
Now, the fourth circuit
eventually decided the case

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:20.460
should remain in state court.

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:22.380
Defendants petition the
Supreme court for review

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:25.460
because the circuit courts
differ on the federal grounds

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:27.590
for removal and whether the
state courts needed to review

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:29.100
every ground for removal.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:32.090
And right now your eyes may
be glazing over with boredom

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:34.130
since this seems like a side skirmish

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:35.430
in a much bigger battle.

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:38.070
And while this is
definitely a technical issue

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:39.110
at the same time

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:41.330
the court's ruling will
certainly impact the speed

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:43.530
at which climate change
cases are reviewed.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:45.490
But it's also possible the
Supreme Court may express

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:47.110
down about the judiciary's rule

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:48.920
in deciding climate change cases.

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:50.590
The Supreme court doesn't
like to decide things,

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:52.120
it doesn't absolutely have to.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:54.300
And this would mean punting
on the substantive issue

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:56.800
so that America's
extremely high-functioning,

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:00.070
very efficient Senate and
House of Representatives

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:02.170
could pass beautiful legislation.

00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:04.550
- I have a feeling it's
going to be beautiful.

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:07.400
- And even if plaintiffs survive
early motions to dismiss,

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:10.790
liability determinations are
very complicated to sort out.

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:12.470
How much fault can you attribute

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:13.860
to one particular defendant?

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:16.410
What happens when all of the
emissions are commingled?

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:18.080
and how do you divvy out the damages

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:19.560
amongst the entire world?

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:20.980
And plaintiffs would have to show

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:22.600
that the defendant's created actual harm

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:23.690
and damages the plaintiffs.

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:25.790
This involves a battle of experts

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:28.570
and real questions about remedies.

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:30.750
And while European litigants
have been successful

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:32.610
in changing actual policy,

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:34.150
the difficulty of determining a remedy

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:36.580
has been prompted many
American courts to question

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:38.900
whether the judicial branch
is really the right forum

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:40.040
for these disputes.

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:41.870
Now, at this point, the question
you're probably asking is,

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:44.140
well, what about international law then?

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:46.120
Well, there's a reason that the U.S.

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:48.020
generally doesn't like to play nice

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:49.630
under international rules.

00:05:49.630 --> 00:05:52.100
But international law is
increasingly playing a major role

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:54.310
on climate disputes with good reason.

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:55.840
For example smaller island nations

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:57.860
are in an especially precarious position.

00:05:57.860 --> 00:05:59.960
The intergovernmental
panel on climate change

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:02.900
reported in 2014 that one
meter of sea level rise

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:05.700
would wipe out 15% of Pacific islands.

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:07.420
And that means the destruction
of many people's homes

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:08.253
and entire cultures.

00:06:08.253 --> 00:06:09.400
For example, Tuvalu,

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:11.600
an Island of approximately 10,000 people

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:14.380
has a high point of only
13 feet above sea level.

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:15.770
Their citizens claim that the actions

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:17.270
of the world's industrialized nations

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:18.990
are slowly flooding the island.

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:21.820
Tuvalu is arguing that some
nations owe them compensation.

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:23.370
And although industrialized nations

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:25.630
make up only 20% of
the world's population,

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:28.687
they account for 60% of the
annual carbon dioxide emissions.

00:06:28.687 --> 00:06:31.120
and America is responsible for 25%

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:34.471
of all global greenhouse gas
emissions all on its own.

00:06:34.471 --> 00:06:36.970
♪ America ♪

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:39.640
So Tuvalu is threatening to
sue the U.S. and Australia

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:41.950
since both are not signatories
to the Kyoto Protocol,

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:43.060
the climate change treaty

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:44.910
that requires lower
greenhouse gas emissions

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:46.900
from industrialized nations.

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:49.430
Now, a claim like that
might conceivably be brought

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:52.100
in the United Nations
International Court of Justice.

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:53.630
Every country that is a member of the U.N.

00:06:53.630 --> 00:06:54.720
is a member of the court,

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:57.060
but not all countries
accept its jurisdiction.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:59.230
The ICJ has two kinds of jurisdiction.

00:06:59.230 --> 00:07:01.220
First, the court has
jurisdiction over disputes

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:02.990
between states that have
accepted the jurisdiction

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:03.823
of the court.

00:07:03.823 --> 00:07:06.277
There are 74 governments that
accept the ICJ's jurisdiction,

00:07:06.277 --> 00:07:08.310
but the U.S. is not one of them.

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:10.260
However, if Tuvalu
wanted to bring a lawsuit

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:12.240
against the country that
was part of the ICJ,

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:13.430
it probably could.

00:07:13.430 --> 00:07:15.770
The ICJ decides cases using
international conventions

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:16.670
and customs.

00:07:16.670 --> 00:07:19.720
Its decisions, however, generally
don't apply as precedent.

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:21.080
And on top of that, the court's decision

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:23.100
binds only the parties
to that particular case

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:23.933
in controversy.

00:07:23.933 --> 00:07:25.680
And the ICJ's second type of jurisdiction

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:27.590
is the ability to give advisory opinions.

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:29.670
The U.N. General Assembly
or the Security Council

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:31.610
may request an opinion from the ICJ.

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:33.630
These opinions would not be binding

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:35.610
but they can influence public policy.

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:38.210
Many Pacific islands are now
pushing for an advisory opinion

00:07:38.210 --> 00:07:40.560
on climate change through the ICJ

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:42.077
but even if they were successful

00:07:42.077 --> 00:07:44.360
that would be a nonbinding decision.

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:45.720
So international lawsuits

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:47.780
are not likely to have a huge effect

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:49.600
on U.S. policy as it stands.

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:50.680
So the bottom line is

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:52.800
that the lawyers are
trying to save the day

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:54.820
but those pesky judges and courts

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:57.040
just keep getting in the way,

00:07:57.040 --> 00:07:59.570
which means that we're
going to have to turn to,

00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:02.550
and I hate to say it, the politicians.

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:04.900
We're going to have to
adopt some new laws.

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:06.560
And not only that, but some of the laws

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:09.470
that we've already adopted
are frankly counterproductive.

00:08:09.470 --> 00:08:12.360
So the question is, what
laws should America pursue?

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:15.120
Well, first we can turn to
some global success stories

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:16.890
regarding greenhouse gas emissions.

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:19.700
For example, Norway has
aggressively targeted gasoline

00:08:19.700 --> 00:08:21.000
and diesel burning cars,

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.070
creating incentives for electric vehicles.

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:25.720
Electric cars now account for
about half of all vehicles

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:26.553
in Norway,

00:08:26.553 --> 00:08:27.800
which you would know if you've ever seen

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:29.720
a GM ad featuring Will Ferrell.

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:33.970
- No way, no way, knock,
knock, it's America.

00:08:33.970 --> 00:08:36.130
And we're gonna punch you in the face.

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:38.510
- Now every major automotive
manufacturer in the world

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:40.760
is developing fleets of plug-in vehicles.

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:43.700
American lawmakers can create
incentives for electric cars.

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:46.360
The European Union banned
the use of hydrofluorocarbons

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:48.850
which are greenhouse gases
used in air conditioners

00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:49.683
and refrigerators.

00:08:49.683 --> 00:08:52.810
The U.S. can and has in the
past taken similar action.

00:08:52.810 --> 00:08:54.920
British Columbia adopted a carbon tax.

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:56.970
It has caused emissions to fall.

00:08:56.970 --> 00:08:59.630
And there have been in the past
times when American citizens

00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:01.710
were willing to pay so called sin taxes,

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:03.530
taxes on booze and cigarettes

00:09:03.530 --> 00:09:06.130
in order to finance things
like sports stadiums.

00:09:06.130 --> 00:09:07.900
Canada and the U.S. agreed in 2016

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:10.750
to curve methane emissions
from oil and gas operations.

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:13.460
The Trump administration rolled
back a lot of those efforts

00:09:13.460 --> 00:09:15.440
but it's possible that
the Biden administration

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:18.480
could unroll back those
efforts if they wanted to.

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:20.210
And while there have been
global success stories

00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:22.990
there have also been some local
state based success stories

00:09:22.990 --> 00:09:24.380
as well here in the United States.

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:26.580
For example, California
has been leading the way

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:29.180
on energy efficient building
standards since the 1970s.

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:30.930
The state standards
continue to get stricter

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:32.730
as new technologies arrive on the scene.

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:34.310
And if these standards were adopted

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:35.143
throughout the United States,

00:09:35.143 --> 00:09:37.370
it would impact emissions
for heating and cooling

00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:38.600
on a nation wide basis.

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:40.030
States like New York and California

00:09:40.030 --> 00:09:42.310
already require utilities
to produce their electricity

00:09:42.310 --> 00:09:43.910
from zero carbon sources.

00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:45.340
That means it's solar, wind, water,

00:09:45.340 --> 00:09:47.810
and potentially nuclear
power are more enticing.

00:09:47.810 --> 00:09:49.140
If the rest of the nation followed suit

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:51.970
it could mean a huge
reduction in CO2 emissions.

00:09:51.970 --> 00:09:53.090
And the New York Times estimates

00:09:53.090 --> 00:09:55.010
that if the U.S. adopted these policies,

00:09:55.010 --> 00:09:57.160
it would quote, slash
greenhouse gas emissions

00:09:57.160 --> 00:10:00.560
in United States by roughly
29% below 2005 levels

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:04.140
by 2025 and roughly 50% by 2050.

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:05.880
And then there are some more controversial

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:08.350
but potentially even more
efficacious policies available

00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:11.310
like cap and trade, which
allows individuals more choice

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:13.030
over how they reduce emissions.

00:10:13.030 --> 00:10:14.950
Now cap and trade as
most people envision it

00:10:14.950 --> 00:10:15.783
is a different method

00:10:15.783 --> 00:10:17.500
than the typical command
and control approach

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:19.940
where the government just simply
sets performance standards

00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:22.640
or forces consumers to pick
electric vehicles for instance.

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:23.900
Cap and trade allows the market

00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:25.760
to determine the price on carbon,

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:27.750
and the price drives investment decisions

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:29.140
and spurs market innovation.

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:30.340
Is this a tax?

00:10:30.340 --> 00:10:32.030
Well, cap and trade differs from tax

00:10:32.030 --> 00:10:33.740
in that it provides a
high level of certainty

00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:34.840
about future emissions

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:36.920
but not about the price
of those emissions,

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:39.820
which leads to a question
about the constitutionality

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:41.410
of a cap and trade policy

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:43.680
because the policy is only as good as

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:45.880
whether it is constitutional
in the first place

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:46.713
to be implemented.

00:10:46.713 --> 00:10:48.010
So while some have questioned

00:10:48.010 --> 00:10:49.840
whether a federal cap and trade program

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:50.840
would be constitutional

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:53.890
because of the limits of the
constitution's Commerce Clause,

00:10:53.890 --> 00:10:56.390
some states have had a
cap and trade program

00:10:56.390 --> 00:10:57.440
for a pretty long time.

00:10:57.440 --> 00:10:58.590
For example, California,

00:10:58.590 --> 00:11:00.820
which has had a cap and
trade program for many years

00:11:00.820 --> 00:11:02.660
and has an agreement with Quebec

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:04.536
through a cross-linked border market,

00:11:04.536 --> 00:11:06.660
the regulated actors in
the two jurisdictions

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:09.300
were able to trade their
allocated admissions allowances.

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:11.010
But the state's program was challenged

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:13.010
by the Trump administration
which basically claimed

00:11:13.010 --> 00:11:15.620
that California couldn't
engage in foreign policy,

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:18.870
that that was reserved solely
for the federal government.

00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:20.730
But so far, California has been successful

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:23.460
at the federal trial level and
has been allowed to continue

00:11:23.460 --> 00:11:24.810
with its particular agreement.

00:11:24.810 --> 00:11:27.110
And then there's the
possibility of truly local

00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:28.450
or state based measures

00:11:28.450 --> 00:11:30.340
or potentially even a federal framework

00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:32.090
to eliminate zoning restrictions

00:11:32.090 --> 00:11:33.590
or softens zoning restrictions

00:11:33.590 --> 00:11:35.810
to allow for greater
density in city centers.

00:11:35.810 --> 00:11:36.750
Because as it happens,

00:11:36.750 --> 00:11:39.070
individuals who live in big dense cities

00:11:39.070 --> 00:11:40.910
tend to produce far less waste

00:11:40.910 --> 00:11:43.220
and produce far fewer
greenhouse gas emissions.

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:44.310
And there are many reasons for it.

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:47.010
Number one, if you live in a big city

00:11:47.010 --> 00:11:49.390
that allows you to walk
around or has public transit

00:11:49.390 --> 00:11:52.000
then you're probably not going
to be polluting via a car.

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:53.730
And you're probably living in a building

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:56.636
that while on an absolute level
is producing more emissions,

00:11:56.636 --> 00:11:58.930
when you divide it by the
sheer number of people

00:11:58.930 --> 00:11:59.763
that are living in it,

00:11:59.763 --> 00:12:02.400
it actually produces far
less on a per person basis.

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:05.480
So the cities tend to
be on a per person basis

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:07.170
far more efficient than the suburbs

00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:08.930
and the rural areas as well.

00:12:08.930 --> 00:12:11.570
And of course, not everyone
wants to move into a big density

00:12:11.570 --> 00:12:12.960
and that's perfectly fine too.

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:14.830
No one needs to force anyone to do it,

00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:16.760
but the truth is a lot
more people want to move

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:18.840
into these big, more efficient cities

00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:21.590
but currently can't because
it's either too expensive

00:12:21.590 --> 00:12:23.430
or there's not enough housing or both.

00:12:23.430 --> 00:12:26.210
And a lot of that is
spurned by the actual laws

00:12:26.210 --> 00:12:27.200
that are currently on the books.

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:28.170
Things like zoning

00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:30.630
that currently limit the
amount of infill development

00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:33.130
that could house more
people more efficiently,

00:12:33.130 --> 00:12:35.740
all the people that want
to move into those cities.

00:12:35.740 --> 00:12:37.200
But let me get down from my soap box.

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:39.210
This video is not intended to be a survey

00:12:39.210 --> 00:12:42.120
of every possible solution
that's out there, far from it.

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:45.420
There's a lot more that
lawyers and normal people

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:47.640
can actually do to help
prevent climate change.

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:49.570
Really, the idea was
just to spark inspiration

00:12:49.570 --> 00:12:51.170
in other professionals who

00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:53.630
you might not assume have a role to play,

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:55.710
but if even the lawyers can get involved

00:12:55.710 --> 00:12:57.810
then I guarantee other people can as well.

00:12:57.810 --> 00:13:00.380
And this video was created in
partnership with Bill Gates,

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:01.727
and inspired by his new book,

00:13:01.727 --> 00:13:03.760
"How to Avoid a Climate Disaster"

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:05.880
You can find out more about
how we can all work together

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:09.216
to avoid a climate disaster
using the link below.

00:13:09.216 --> 00:13:12.383
